Compose a memorial of congratulation on behalf of the assembled officials, on the occasion when the countries south of the sea each dispatched envoys to present qilin, four in all. 〔Eighth year of Xuande (1433)〕

Legal judgments 〔five topics〕:
Officials’ hereditary succession and protection privileges;
Lands of meritorious officials;
The district drinking ceremony;
Preferential relief for military dependents;
Repair of granaries and storehouses.

Third Session
Policy questions 〔five〕
Question: From antiquity, emperors and kings who preserved the difficult and weighty undertaking of rulership and brought their splendid government to greatness invariably benefited from mutual admonition between ruler and ministers. When an enlightened ruler and worthy ministers met, united in virtue and purpose, there were injunctions concerning timely attention to incipient affairs [?], counsels of reverence, and warnings against ease. Such examples celebrated in the Book of Odes and the Book of Documents are too numerous to enumerate. Can you discuss and explain them?

